-      <div class="panel-body"><div class="attack-panel"><div class="attack-name">Disclosure (2010)</div><div class="attack-diff">The only known "break"</div><p class="attack-desc">Despite four decades of public challenge ciphertexts and serious attention from professional cryptanalysts, no published cryptanalytic break of Chaocipher exists. The cipher was "broken" only when its algorithm was finally disclosed by the Byrne family in 2010 — vindicating Byrne's belief while also confirming Kerckhoffs' principle: any cipher can be defeated if the algorithm becomes known and the key is short.</p></div><div class="attack-panel"><div class="attack-name">Known-Plaintext Attack</div><div class="attack-diff">Complexity: Moderate (now that algorithm is public)</div><p class="attack-desc">Modern researchers have shown that with sufficient known plaintext (a few hundred characters), the initial wheel orderings can be recovered by tracing back the deterministic permutations. Pure ciphertext-only attacks remain difficult.</p></div></div>
+      <div class="panel-body"><div class="attack-panel"><div class="attack-name">Disclosure (2010)</div><div class="attack-diff">Algorithm disclosure enabled public analysis</div><p class="attack-desc">The Byrne family donated the Chaocipher papers to the National Cryptologic Museum in 2010, making the algorithm public after 92 years. Disclosure itself was not a cryptanalytic break; it allowed researchers to study the keyed construction.</p></div><div class="attack-panel"><div class="attack-name">Known-Plaintext and In-Depth Attacks</div><div class="attack-diff">Published key-recovery attacks</div><p class="attack-desc">A known-plaintext key-recovery method was published in 2010. In 2016, George Lasry, Moshe Rubin, Nils Kopal, and Arno Wacker published a hill-climbing attack against multiple messages encrypted with the same initial settings, plus a known-plaintext attack on short in-depth messages. Their <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/01611194.2015.1091797" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cryptologia paper</a> establishes weaknesses in the classic cipher; it does not claim a general ciphertext-only break of every isolated message.</p></div></div>
