-      <p>The KL-7, code-named ADONIS, was the rotor machine that carried NATO’s most sensitive traffic for almost two decades. It encrypted everything from US Navy submarine orders to NATO command messages. By 1965 the United States alone had over 25,000 KL-7 units in service.</p><p style="margin-top:1rem;">Cryptanalytically, the KL-7 was never broken. Operationally, it suffered the most damaging spy compromise in modern American history: Navy Chief Warrant Officer John Walker sold its key lists to the Soviets from 1968 to 1985, allowing the KGB to read US Navy traffic in near real time.</p>
+      <p>The KL-7, code-named ADONIS, was the rotor machine that carried NATO’s most sensitive traffic for almost two decades. It encrypted everything from US Navy submarine orders to NATO command messages. By 1965 the United States alone had over 25,000 KL-7 units in service.</p><p style="margin-top:1rem;">No public cryptanalytic break of operational KL-7 traffic is documented. Operationally, it suffered the most damaging spy compromise in modern American history: Navy Chief Warrant Officer John Walker sold its key lists to the Soviets from 1968 to 1985, allowing the KGB to read US Navy traffic in near real time.</p>
